1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:13,471
This picture begins with rotating circles. Each circle supplies a vector with a measured radius, a rotation frequency, and a starting phase. Add those vectors, and the final tip becomes the drawing pen.

2
00:00:15,208 --> 00:00:31,784
Here is one smaller contour, enlarged. Its sixty four terms include one fixed offset and sixty three rotating circles. Large circles establish the shape. Small circles refine the detail. Negative frequencies rotate in the opposite direction.

3
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:47,836
Now all six hundred fifty one chains run together. You are seeing all forty one thousand thirteen rotating circles. Their tips trace the stored contours at two thousand forty eight samples per orbit. These outlines will define the portrait.

4
00:00:49,542 --> 00:01:04,539
A closed outline tells us which pixels lie inside it. The nonzero winding test creates that region. Each stored sign then adds or subtracts its tone increment. Subtracting regions preserves holes and interior details.

5
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:10,709
The first tone layer establishes the broad silhouette and its darkest structure.

6
00:01:12,417 --> 00:01:16,235
The second layer begins separating the figure from the surrounding field.

7
00:01:17,958 --> 00:01:22,268
The third layer adds another increment wherever its signed regions apply.

8
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,949
The fourth layer continues the build. Every earlier contribution remains in place.

9
00:01:30,667 --> 00:01:34,656
The fifth layer brings in more intermediate tones across the portrait.

10
00:01:36,375 --> 00:01:40,919
The sixth layer adds the next set of closed contours to the accumulated field.

11
00:01:42,625 --> 00:01:47,233
The seventh layer continues the tonal separation around the face and clothing.

12
00:01:48,958 --> 00:01:53,460
The eighth layer adds finer regions while retaining the larger shapes underneath.

13
00:01:55,167 --> 00:02:00,052
The ninth layer contributes eighty nine signed outlines, the largest group in this build.

14
00:02:01,792 --> 00:02:06,165
The tenth layer adds another set of regions to the same scalar tone field.

15
00:02:07,875 --> 00:02:11,651
The eleventh layer brings the build closer to its brightest tone bands.

16
00:02:13,375 --> 00:02:18,282
The twelfth layer adds the next highlight regions without replacing the earlier layers.

17
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,013
The thirteenth layer completes the contour based tone field. The portrait is now assembled.

18
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:42,174
The lightning is calculated separately. Fifteen paths use oscillating offsets to form the branches. Distance from each sampled path sets the glow, and the fracture envelope controls its strength. That light field is added to the portrait tone.

19
00:02:43,875 --> 00:02:56,376
The tone passes through a sigmoid. Twenty nine cosine waves then add the specified grain. This texture is deterministic too. The same equations produce the same pattern every time.

20
00:02:58,083 --> 00:03:10,030
Finally, the supplied Perron vector controls the red, green, and blue channels. Each channel blends the shade with its cube. That turns the single brightness field into this warm gold palette.

21
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:19,750
Circles become contours. Contours build tone. Lightning, grain, and colour complete the image. The equations are the picture.

22
00:03:21,458 --> 00:03:29,501
Mona Tesla, version five. Formula artwork and coefficients by Jeromie Beasley. Narration generated with Kokoro.
